Hi!

First many thanks to Dr. Tom Blinn, Thomas Leitner
Neil Dyce and Stuart McKenzie .

Sometimes it is very helpfull to read the f***ing RELEASE Notes.
I had to run the ECU utility and to enable VGA.
Now CDE runs fine, without any problems.

Original answers:

Dr. Tom Blinn:

Tru64 UNIX V5.0 *should* support the onboard VGA-Graphics on that system,BUT
you have to make sure that the graphics is configured correctly.  In many of
the older EISA bus systems, if you don't update the SRM console  firmware
AND update the EISA configuration database (with the ECU that isrun from a
floppy disk), then the kernel won't recognize the graphics andthings won't
work.  Since the graphics was apparenly not recognized when you did the
installation (I'm assuming you didn't get a graphical installbut did it in
character cell mode), you may have to reinstall once you getthe graphics
working correctly in the console.

Niel Dyce:

The console environment variable should be set to "graphics" rather
than"serial". However you may need to run the EISA configuration utility
toenable the VGA graphics controller. This is described in the release
notesfor 4.0D which should be on the web (www.unix.digital.com), if you
don'thave a corresponding section in your copy.


Stuart McKenzie:

Well we are running CDE on a 1000 4/200 system with 4.0D and on-board Cirrus
card.  I doubt if v5 is different.  What you have to watch out foris that
installing 4.0D and I think 5 upsets the BIOS or whatever DEC callthe system
level and you need to use the floppy at system level to resetuse of the
cirrus card.

Thomas Leitner:

Eine recht billige und gute Grafikkarte ist die Elsa Gloria Synergy. Die
kommt mit 8MB Video Ram und kann bis 1280x1024 bei 24bpp und 75Hz.  Die
Karte sollte so um 300-400 DM zu kriegen sein.

"The Elsa Gloria Synergy is a good and cheap graphics card. It come with
8 MB Video RAM and a resolution of 1280x1024x24bpp at 75Hz.
Price 200-400 German Mark about 150-205 Euro."


