Thanks to all for your help.  The original post is at the end of this
message.  To clarify, I was attempting a remote X session, logging in as
root but the session was being refused.  The /etc/securettys file was
the key.

Thanks to Kevin Criss who suggested checking the
/var/adm/syslog.dated/current/auth.log file to determine the 'exact
form' of the display being denied remote access.  In this case, the
display was of the form 'pc.full.domain:display#' (eg.
host1.my.domain:0).  It was then a simple matter of adding this same
display entry to the /etc/securettys file.

Also thanks to Joerg Bruehe for the following suggestion:
1) Enter 'pc' as well as 'pc.full.domain' to prevent against different
granularity in address resolving.
2) Enter the IP address as well to prepare for non-working name lookup.

