system, the device file does points to the cdrom, and the kernel does
support CDFS. My problem is that I can mount cdrom successfully only
once after each reboot. 

Dr. Tom Blinn give some good suggestions and I will try these at late
time.

"If it were my system, the first thing I'd do is replace the CDROM drive
to rule out a bad drive.  If the symptoms don't go away with a different
CDROM drive, then I'd try different media (but I assume you've already
done that). There are many ways media can be damaged and made
unreadable.  If neither the drive nor the media swaps make the problem
go away, I'd try booting the original V4.0D kit CDROM and see if I can
read that media reliably.  If I had a spare hard drive (I do have lots
of spares), I'd install the vanilla V4.0D product (no patches) onto my
spare disk, then exercise the CDROM drive to see if the symptoms go away
(either confirming or ruling out a bad patch)."

