<DIV><FONT face=3DArial size=3D2>Forcing Oracle to use 8 MB-aligned =
shared memory=20
segments.<BR>&nbsp;<BR>This note was written by Elizabeth Sperling and =
Glenn=20
Stasse of the <BR>Washington, D.C. Benchmark Center. It addresses some =
setup=20
features when<BR>running Oracle7 on Digital UNIX/Alpha systems. It is =
applicable=20
to<BR>Digital UNIX Version 3.x systems with the OSF350-055 patch =
installed=20
and<BR>to Digital UNIX version 3.2D systems.=20
<BR>&nbsp;<BR>Introduction:<BR>&nbsp;<BR>An optimization has been =
developed for=20
the use of large, shared memory<BR>segments on Digital Unix V3.x. This=20
optimization involves the use of<BR>shared page table entries(PTEs) to =
map=20
shared memory segments and the<BR>use of an Alpha&nbsp; architectural =
feature=20
called granularity hints. When<BR>this patch is applied and&nbsp; the =
shared=20
memory segments are properly<BR>aligned, each process attaching to the =
shared=20
segments will use the<BR>shared PTEs to map the shared segments, =
reducing memory=20
consumption and<BR>improving performance, particularly when attaching =
and=20
detaching to the<BR>shared segments. <BR>&nbsp;<BR>A by-product of this=20
optimization is that the shared memory segments<BR>need to&nbsp; be =
aligned on=20
8MB boundaries (that is, the lower 23 bits of the<BR>address must =
be&nbsp; zero)=20
in order to function properly. Non-aligned<BR>segments will bypass =
the&nbsp;=20
shared PTE mechanism and revert to the<BR>traditional virtual memory=20
system.<BR>&nbsp;<BR>This note addresses one method found to be =
successful in=20
size-aligning<BR>the shared memory usage of Oracle7 such that the Oracle =
shared=20
memory<BR>usage is falls on an even 8MB boundary. This note does NOT =
address=20
the<BR>need for a shared segment's attach address to be aligned.=20
<BR>&nbsp;<BR>Setting a value for Granularity Hints for shared memory=20
segments:<BR>&nbsp;<BR>The granularity hints fix makes use of the =
granularity=20
hints bits in the<BR>page table entry (PTE) as specified in the Alpha=20
Architecture.<BR>Granularity hints is a Translation Buffer (TB) =
optimization=20
that allows<BR>the TB to map more than a single page. Enabling the =
granularity=20
hints<BR>fix also enables the shared PTE fix. The granularity hints fix=20
is<BR>enabled by setting the "gh-chunks" parameter in OSF=20
/etc/sysconfigtab.<BR>The gh-chunks parameter is the number of 4 =
Megabyte chunks=20
reserved at<BR>boot time for shared memory use. <BR>&nbsp;<BR>In=20
/etc/sysconfigtab set the value for gh-chunks according to the =
size<BR>of=20
your&nbsp; shared memory requirements. The entry should be similar to=20
the<BR>following:=20
<BR>&nbsp;<BR>vm:<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=20
gh-chunks=3D512<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=
&nbsp;&nbsp;=20
OR<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=20
gh-chunks=3D0x200<BR>&nbsp;<BR>This reserves 2 Gigabytes of physical =
memory for=20
shared memory.&nbsp; The<BR>calculation is:=20
<BR>&nbsp;<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In =
decimal:&nbsp;=20
4194304 * 512 =3D =
2147483648<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In=20
Hex:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 0x400000 * 0x200 =3D=20
0x80000000<BR>&nbsp;<BR>Please Note: Memory that is set aside at boot =
time for=20
shared memory by<BR>specifying a value for gh-chunks will not be used =
for=20
